<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=thermodynamics">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thermodynamics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thermodynamics is the branch of physics dealing with heat, work and temperature and their relation to energy, entropy and the properties of matter. It developed out of a desire to increase the efficiency of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a>, and Sadi Carnot's Reflections on the Motive Power of Fire (1824) marked its start as a modern science. Rudolf Clausius first stated the second law in 1850 and named entropy in 1865, and William Thomson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-kelvin">Lord Kelvin</a>, gave a concise definition of thermodynamics in 1854. William Rankine wrote the first textbook in 1859, and statistical thermodynamics was set out by physicists including Maxwell, Boltzmann, Planck, Clausius and Gibbs.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Clement Greenberg placed it in mid-19th-century France, with Charles Baudelaire in literature and Manet in painting; William Everdell dated it to the 1870s, with Richard Dedekind’s mathematics and Ludwig Boltzmann’s statistical <b>thermodynamics</b>, and in painting to Georges Seurat’s Divisionism of 1885–1886.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1824</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-leonard-sadi-carnot">Nicolas Léonard Sadi Carnot</a> · Rudolf Clausius · William Thomson</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Its history as a discipline is said to begin with Otto von Guericke's vacuum pump of 1650; the first and second laws emerged together in the 1850s from the work of Rankine, Clausius and Thomson.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">Steam engine<span>1606</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/vacuum-pump">Vacuum pump<span>1650</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=thermodynamics">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
